@page "/projects/vam"

<ProjectPage Title="Väm"
             Kicker="Soft launch · 2026"
             AccentClass="accent-red"
             Summary="Built a phone-first map of where Gothenburg's food trucks are today, with every location confirmed by hand and a design that needs about one database query a minute per region, by entering locations from a phone and serving every visitor from one cached endpoint."
             Tech="@(new[] { "TypeScript", "Next.js 16", "React 19", "Supabase Postgres", "MapLibre GL JS", "Protomaps", "Tailwind CSS v4", "Vitest", "Vercel" })"
             Links="@(new[] { new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Visit the site", "https://vararmaten.vercel.app"), new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Source on GitHub", "https://github.com/Ollie1994/whereisfood") })">

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        Väm is short for <em lang="sv">Var är maten</em>, "where's the food". Gothenburg's trucks
        announce where they'll be on Instagram and Facebook, one post or Story at a time,
        and there has been no central map since Streetkäk shut down in 2017. Väm is that
        map, with no login and no tracking. The interface is in Swedish.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>It's in production as a soft launch.</strong> Visitors see a page about the
        project, and the map itself is shown only to the admin while locations are entered
        and tested. It opens to everyone when the trucks are back for the season: a map
        with two trucks on it helps nobody. Phase 10 of 11 is done.
    </p>

    <h2>The version I threw away</h2>

    <p>
        The first design was automatic: webhooks and inbound email were to feed a Swedish
        caption parser, with a confidence score on each location. I built the ingestion
        lanes and most of the parser, and dropped the design before joining them.
    </p>

    <p>
        A parser can't see a post that is only an image, a Story, or a schedule posted as a
        graphic, and it can misread the posts it does see. The code is archived, and none of
        that design is left in the app.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            The rule that decided it: <strong>a wrong pin costs more than a missing
            one</strong>. The replacement is deliberately low-tech: one admin, entering every
            location by hand from a phone.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Making manual entry fast</h2>

    <p>
        Entering everything by hand only works if it's quick. A pin comes from a tap
        on the map, a known spot, a recently used place or the phone's GPS. Start and end
        times are pre-filled and always required. A morning checklist suggests what each
        truck did on the same weekday recently, and nothing reaches the map until the
        admin confirms it.
    </p>

    <p>
        The map is equally plain about what it knows. Only trucks with an entry today
        appear, and each one says when its entry was last updated and, where the truck has
        one, links to its Instagram so you can check.
    </p>

    <h2>One cached read path</h2>

    <p>
        The browser never talks to the database. The map polls a single endpoint every 60
        seconds while the tab is visible, and the CDN caches that response for 60 seconds.
        In normal use that means about one database query a minute per region, however
        many people have the map open.
    </p>

    <p>
        The public API roles have no access to the truck and location tables at all, so
        nobody can pull a truck's location history through the database's own API. Map
        tiles are a self-hosted Protomaps file rather than a hosted tile service.
    </p>

    <h2>Rules the database enforces</h2>

    <p>
        Two overlapping entries for the same truck are refused by a Postgres
        <strong>exclusion constraint</strong>, not by application code that might forget to
        check. Every table has row-level security and explicit grants.
    </p>

    <p>
        The tests for those guards are themselves checked. Over sixty small SQL files each
        remove or weaken something in the schema, and for most of them a test has to fail.
        A few check instead that another guard already covers the change, or that the
        tests' own setup refuses to run.
    </p>

    <h2>A locked-down admin</h2>

    <p>
        The admin signs in with an emailed link and then a code from an authenticator app,
        and a session is refused twelve hours after sign-in. Every admin route checks the
        session, the role and the second factor again for itself, and the admin pages get a
        content security policy with a new nonce per request.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>

    <p>
        Strictly layered: route handlers speak HTTP, services hold the logic, and only one
        layer touches Postgres. Every pull request runs type checking, unit tests,
        integration tests against a local Supabase, a production build with a smoke check,
        a dependency audit, a secret scan and CodeQL.
    </p>

    <p>
        I build it with Claude Code under a process that is part of the project: plans with
        numbered decisions so nothing is argued twice, and a log of every time the process
        itself let a mistake through.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
